course: dictionary
lesson: 5307
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 1
explain: Some words belong to a religion, and some have a looser meaning in everyday talk. Karma is both. Context clues tell you which meaning is being used. «Hindus believe that what we do has consequences (karma) and that souls are born again and again until they reach freedom (moksha).» The brackets, the word Hindus and the word moksha show this is a religious idea.
explain: «Hindus believe in karma: good and bad actions bring their own results.» The colon tells you what karma means. It is the belief that what you do, good or bad, comes back to you. Try it in the sentence: «Hindus believe in the belief that good and bad actions bring results.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: In everyday talk, karma is used more loosely. «That's karma!» is often said when someone who behaved badly has bad luck. Nobody is making a serious religious point. Ask yourself: is this sentence about a religion, or is it a light comment about someone's luck?
fact: Karma means the idea that what a person does, good or bad, brings results back to them.
fact: In Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism and Sikhism, karma is taught together with rebirth, the idea that souls are born again.
fact: Phrases you may hear include «good karma», «bad karma», «instant karma» and «karma will get you».
fact: In everyday English, people often say «That's karma!» as a joke when someone who did wrong has bad luck.
exercise: «Hindus believe in karma: good and bad actions bring their own results.» What does karma mean here? | A rule that only grown-ups may follow | *The idea that your actions, good or bad, bring results back to you | A tree that grows in India | A special food eaten at festivals
exercise: In «Hindus believe in karma», find and copy the word that tells you karma is a belief. | in | *believe | Hindus | karma
exercise: Maya says «That's karma!» after a boy who was rude to everyone trips in the mud. What does she mean? | *The bad luck is the result of how he behaved | Maya is cold and hungry | The boy has been given a prize for good work | It is a special Hindu festival
test: «Karma will get you» is a warning. What does it suggest? | Karma is a kind of fish that lives in rivers | Rude people always win prizes | *If you behave badly, bad things may come back to you
test: Which phrase means that good things come back to someone who behaved well? | «servo» | «dreich» | *«good karma» | «bad karma»
exam: Why does the word karma have two meanings? | It is two different words joined together by accident | *Religion uses it for a belief, and everyday talk uses it loosely | Every country has a different meaning for it | It means exactly the same as luck
